WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.019
A record-breaking 22 million comments
have been filed with the FCC on its

00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:14.670
process to restore Internet freedom, the
so-called Title II net neutrality docket.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:19.439
What does this number mean? Well, as a
matter of policy, not much. FCC

00:00:19.439 --> 00:00:22.800
commissioners are politically appointed,
and are supposed to be outside of the

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.550
influence of others. As a matter of
procedure, the FCC has to allow for

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.720
public comment. But these comments should
not be confused with votes. And even if

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.980
they were, they'd be dramatically skewed
in favor of the digital elite, not truly

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.879
reflective of the will of the people.
Data shows that net neutrality advocacy

00:00:38.879 --> 00:00:43.440
is driven largely by males, age 21 to 34,
who earn at least seventy six thousand

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.680
dollars a year. These digital elites can
afford to intellectualize their

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.700
preferences and impose them on others,
but for underprivileged persons

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.149
these rules perpetuate the digital
divide. Rather than digital elites

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:57.660
dictating how the Internet should be,
Internet freedom is about empowering

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:02.129
individuals – the poor, the old, and people
of color – to get online quickly, easily

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:07.710
and at the lowest cost. That's why we
should support Internet freedom.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.979
to learn more about my take on Title II net
neutrality, check the links in the

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:15.450
description below. Also, let us know what
other topics you want our scholars to

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:20.620
cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

